@container lets a component style itself based on its *container's* size instead of the viewport, making truly reusable components. The parent needs container-type: inline-size to establish the query context.

```css
.card-host { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 420px) {
  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 96px 1fr; }
}
```

```js
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(field, message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';  // appears in stack traces
    this.field = field;
    this.status = 400;
  }
}

class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(status, body) {
    super(`API responded with ${status}`);
    this.name = 'ApiError';
    this.status = status;
    this.body = body;
  }
}

function validateEmail(email) {
  if (!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
    throw new ValidationError('email', 'Invalid email format');
  }
}

async function saveUser(data) {
  try {
    validateEmail(data.email);  // sync throw
    const res = await fetch('/api/users', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(data) });
    if (!res.ok) throw new ApiError(res.status, await res.text());
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
      showFieldError(err.field, err.message); // recoverable: flag the input
      return null;
    }
    throw err; // unknown: rethrow, log at the top level
  } finally {
    console.log('runs on success, catch, AND rethrow');
  }
}
```
Extend `Error` and call `super(message)` so you get proper stacks and `instanceof` still works. Use distinct error types to separate handling decisions (retry a 503, flag a 400 field, crash on genuine bugs). `finally` runs no matter how the block exits — ideal for closing loaders or connections. Never swallow errors silently; catch only what you can meaningfully handle and rethrow the rest.

```js
// Spread: expands iterables/objects
const a = [1, 2, 3];
const b = [...a, 4, 5];              // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] (copy + extend)
const copy = [...a];                 // shallow copy
console.log(Math.max(...a));         // 3 (spread into function args)

const base = { theme: 'dark', lang: 'en' };
const overrides = { lang: 'fa' };
const config = { ...base, ...overrides }; // later keys win -> { theme: 'dark', lang: 'fa' }

// Rest: collects the remainder
const [first, ...others] = b;        // first = 1, others = [2, 3, 4, 5]
const { theme, ...rest } = config;   // theme = 'dark', rest = { lang: 'fa' }

function sum(...numbers) {           // rest parameter: a real array
  return numbers.reduce((t, n) => t + n, 0);
}
console.log(sum(1, 2, 3, 4));        // 10
```
- Spread (`...`) writes: it expands an array/object into another literal or arguments into a call. It makes shallow copies only — nested objects are still shared.
- Rest (`...`) reads: it gathers remaining elements/properties into a fresh array/object, or collects function arguments.
Same three dots, opposite directions: spread unpacks, rest packs.

