<main><h1>Themed page</h1><p>Colors come from custom properties.</p></main>
```

```css
:root {
  --bg: #0b0f14; --text: #e6edf3; --surface: #151b23; --accent: #2dd4bf;
}
[data-theme="light"] {
  --bg: #f8fafc; --text: #0f172a; --surface: #ffffff; --accent: #0d9488;
}
body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; transition: background .3s, color .3s; }
button { background: var(--accent); color: var(--bg); border: 0; padding: .6rem 1.2rem; border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer; }
```

```js
const btn = document.querySelector('#theme');
const saved = localStorage.getItem('theme');
if (saved) document.documentElement.dataset.theme = saved;
btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const next = document.documentElement.dataset.theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light';
  document.documentElement.dataset.theme = next;
  localStorage.setItem('theme', next);
});
```

**Why it works:** all colors flow from custom properties, so switching themes only swaps the variable values on `:root` via the `data-theme` attribute — no restyling of individual elements. The choice is persisted in `localStorage` and applied *before* first paint (script runs on load), avoiding a flash of the wrong theme.

